SUMMARY=[heading('Summary'),paragraph('A function z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables x and y if a unique z is obtained from each ordered pair (x,y). The real numbers x and y are independent variables; z is dependent. The domain is the set of ordered pairs for which f(x,y) is real, and the range is the set of all values of f(x,y).'),paragraph('A polynomial function in x and y is the sum of functions of the form:'),eq(r'f(x,y)=Cx^my^n'),paragraph('If z=f(x,y) is a function of two variables, its first partial derivatives with respect to x and y are defined by:'),eq(r'f_x(x,y)=\lim_{\Delta x\to0}\frac{f(x+\Delta x,y)-f(x,y)}{\Delta x},\quad f_y(x,y)=\lim_{\Delta y\to0}\frac{f(x,y+\Delta y)-f(x,y)}{\Delta y}'),paragraph('A function f(x,y) is homogeneous of degree n in x and y if for all values x,y and every positive λ:'),eq(r'f(\lambda x,\lambda y)=\lambda^nf(x,y)'),paragraph('The specialty of Euler’s theorem is to verify the degree of a homogeneous function. The homogeneous function z=f(x,y) is not altered if x,y are stretched or squeezed by any real scalar λ.'),note('Polynomial exponents m,n are nonnegative integers in this definition. The partial limits must exist in the appropriate domain. Homogeneous values scale by λⁿ rather than staying unchanged unless n=0; positive scaling and domain conditions matter. Euler’s identity is xfₓ+yfᵧ=nf for differentiable homogeneous functions.')]
HISTORY=[heading('History'),figure('partial308-neumann','Carl Neumann (1832–1925)','Source portrait of Carl Neumann.'),paragraph('Carl Neumann was German mathematician. He studied physics from his father and later became a mathematician. His father was professor at Konigsberg university. In 1875 be introduce the new standard notation (d-hat) during a lecture on the mechanical theory of heat. The symbol was popularized by his name as Neumann notation or δ (Greek delta). He worked on the drichlet principle and can be considered one of the initiators of the theory of integral equations. The Neumann series, that is analogous to the geometric series:'),eq(r'\frac1{1-x}=a+x+x^2+x^3+\ldots'),paragraph('is named after him. Carl Neumann also founded a mathematical research journal mathematische annalen. The Neumann boundary condition for different types of ordinary differential equations and partial differential equation is also named after him. He also developed an interest in thermodynamics via the overlap of heat and electricity.'),note('The history wording and claims are source transcription, not independently verified history. The displayed geometric series requires the constant term 1, rather than arbitrary a, and convergence |x|<1: 1/(1−x)=1+x+x²+… .')]
